---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import PngToIco from '@/components/tools/image-convert/PngToIco.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="png-to-ico">
  <PngToIco client:load mode="encode" />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>What an .ico file really is</h2>
    <p>
      An ICO is a container, not an image. It starts with a six-byte directory header, followed by a
      sixteen-byte entry per image (width, height, colour depth, byte length and offset), and then
      the image payloads themselves. That is why a single favicon.ico can hold a 16-pixel version
      drawn for clarity and a 256-pixel version with full detail, and why every consumer picks the
      entry that suits it best rather than scaling one image on the fly.
    </p>

    <h2>Which size is used where</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Size</th><th>Used by</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>16 × 16</td><td>Browser tab, address bar, bookmark lists, history</td></tr>
          <tr><td>24 × 24</td><td>Older Internet Explorer favourites bar</td></tr>
          <tr><td>32 × 32</td><td>Taskbar, high-DPI browser tabs, desktop shortcuts</td></tr>
          <tr><td>48 × 48</td><td>Windows desktop and Explorer medium icons</td></tr>
          <tr><td>64 / 128</td><td>Intermediate scaling steps on high-DPI displays</td></tr>
          <tr><td>256 × 256</td><td>Explorer “extra large icons”, Alt-Tab, jump lists</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The default selection (16, 32, 48 and 256) covers all those cases with a file that is usually
      well under 30 KB. All seven sizes do no harm, but the file is slightly larger.
    </p>

    <h2>PNG payloads, not BMP</h2>
    <p>
      Historically each entry inside an ICO was an uncompressed BMP-DIB with a separate one-bit
      transparency mask. Windows Vista added support for PNG payloads, and every browser and Windows
      version since reads them. We write PNG for a simple reason: a 256 × 256 BMP entry is 256 KB of
      raw pixels, while the same image as PNG is usually a few kilobytes. If you need to support
      Windows XP, leave 256 out of the selection. XP still finds the smaller
      entries it understands.
    </p>

    <h2>Designing an icon that survives 16 pixels</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Simplify. A 16 × 16 grid holds 256 pixels in total. Wordmarks, gradients and thin outlines all vanish; a single bold glyph or shape does not.</li>
      <li>Crop the padding. Logos usually ship with generous whitespace. Trim it before uploading here, otherwise your icon is a small mark floating in an empty square.</li>
      <li>Maximise contrast. The icon will be seen against both a light and a dark browser theme. A mid-grey mark disappears against one of them.</li>
      <li>Start at 512 or 1024 px. Downscaling from a large clean source with a Lanczos filter, as this tool does, gives far better small sizes than upscaling from a 64 px original.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Non-square logos</h2>
    <p>
      Icons are square by definition. With padding switched on, a wide logo is centred in a square
      canvas: transparent by default, or filled with a colour if you type a hex value. With padding
      off, the image is stretched to a square, which distorts nearly everything. If your brand mark
      is strongly horizontal, consider making a separate square version (an initial, a symbol) rather
      than squeezing the full logo into a favicon.
    </p>

    <h2>Installing the favicon</h2>
    <p>
      Put the file at the root of your site as <code>/favicon.ico</code>. Browsers request that exact
      path even when no link tag is present, which is why it is still the most reliable fallback.
      Then add the explicit tags (the tool gives them to you to copy), so you can also offer a scalable
      SVG icon and an Apple touch icon for iOS home screens. If the old icon persists after you
      deploy, that is browser caching: try a hard reload, or a private window, before assuming
      something is wrong.
    </p>

    <h2>Built here, not on someone's server</h2>
    <p>
      To convert PNG to ICO, the resize, the PNG encoding and the ICO directory assembly all happen in
      this tab, so your unreleased brand mark is not in someone's temporary directory. There is also no
      queue: change the size selection and the icon rebuilds at once.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/ico-to-png">ICO to PNG</a> for the reverse direction,
      <a href="/favicon-generator">Favicon Generator</a> for the full set of PNG sizes and the web
      app manifest, and <a href="/resize-image">Resize Image</a> if you need to prepare the source
      first.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
